Core Logic in C/C++: Write the core computational parts of your application in C/C++. These could be functions or classes that are performance-sensitive or need access to system resources. Functionality: Make sure the C/C++ code is modular and exposes functions that can be easily called from another language. For example, you might write a set of mathematical or data processing functions in C++. Example C++ Code: // mymath.cpp
# include <iostream> extern "C" { // A simple C++ function int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
} The` extern "C" block ensures that the C++ code is callable from C``, making it easier for SWIG or other tools to generate wrappers.

